---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Per Diem Data 2026: Who Builds Trip Expense Ledger';
const description = 'Trip Expense Ledger is published by Radif Partners: free 2026 per diem, mileage and fuel calculators built on GSA, IRS, EIA and EPA files, no account.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Trip Expense Ledger</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Filling in a travel voucher should not take three browser tabs: the GSA lookup for the lodging cap, a second page for the meal breakdown, a third for the mileage rate, then a spreadsheet to apply the 75% rule and subtract the conference lunch. This site does better than the first Google result for per diem and mileage searches because it computes the full reimbursement of a trip in one screen, with the GSA FY2027 tables and the IRS rate of {K.centsWord(K.irs('2026-07-01'))} in force since July 1, 2026, where gsa.gov gives one rate per city and leaves the trip, the provided meals and the miles to the traveler.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators on public data. It is not a travel management company, an expense app, an employer or a government office, and it has no commercial link with any of them. No page asks for an e-mail address, no tool needs an account, and there is no advertising on the site today.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What the calculators rest on</h2>
    <p>The lodging and M&IE figures are the GSA files themselves, both fiscal years, rebuilt by a script: {K.T.dest.length} area records with twelve monthly lodging maximums each and {K.num(K.ZIP_COUNT)} ZIP codes. Census files place {K.num(K.PLACE_COUNT)} cities in their county so that a search by city name finds the right area. Day rules come from the Federal Travel Regulation as rewritten in December 2025, mileage from the IRS notice and announcement of 2026, the high-low and transportation rates from IRS Notice 2026-60, fuel prices from the Energy Information Administration and vehicle consumption from the EPA. Where no official figure exists, as for the yearly loss of value of a car or the price at a public charger, the tool asks you for it and says so.</p>
    <h2>How the site is checked</h2>
    <p>Each rule is coded once and tested against the official examples: the GSA first and last day amounts of every tier, a trip across September 30, the July 1 mileage break, the OCONUS breakdown table from $1 to $265. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists the rules and the tests; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how errors are corrected.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>A figure that looks wrong, a newer release, a state rule worth adding: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or the <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact page</a>. We cannot read your employer's travel policy or approve a voucher, but every message about a rate is checked against its source.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
